WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.490 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  so I was watching Batman vs Superman for  some reason which I guess isn't  entertaining enough film albeit not  cinematic masterpiece by any stretch but  as I was watching it I noticed something  very strange that took me so much by  surprise that I had to discuss it with  my friends afterwards after we had  watched it now it takes place right at  the end of the film so obviously if you  haven't seen the film I'm gonna go into  heavy spoiler territory here it's right  at the climax of the film when Superman  Batman and Wonder Woman have teamed up  to fight this monster there's a lot of  action a lot of special effects a lot of  just a lot and then it evolves into a  point where Superman feels like he has  to sacrifice himself in order to save  the others and the rest of the city and  mankind basically which he then does the  bad guy die of Superman is dying it's  all very sad bla bla bla but then we get  to this scene do you see you too isn't  that a cross it's kind of prominent in  the shot too seriously think about it  Superman has just sacrificed his own  life to save mankind and right at that  moment there is a cross slapped on the  screen is Superman Jesus is that the  symbolism that's going on here now I  don't know if I completely overanalyzing  this was this on purpose was it a  complete coincidence or some mysterious  option in between these two  I mean Christian symbolism in movies and  popular culture isn't unusual we all  know that Narnia is about Jesus for  example I'm not sure about Zack Snyder's  religious conviction I don't really feel  like looking it up right now but could  it be that they consciously made a very  clear Jesus reference in this superhero  movie it's interesting to think about  how our religious traditions have  affected our societies to such a large  extent even if the cross is coincidental  the story is very reminiscent of the  story of Jesus in the Bible it is after  all one of the most famous stories of  all time and one that is so ingrained  into our sort of cultural consciousness  that we don't even really think about it  when it comes to many Western countries  we like to look at ourselves as very  secularized here in Sweden where I live  we're often viewed as the most secular  country in the world and there may be  some truth to this from a certain  perspective but at the same time  Christianity has been the dominant  religion here for so long and che  so much of our traditions that its  presence is inescapable regardless if we  want to or not our society is completely  infused with Christianity its present in  our moral values how we treat each other  the elderly the poor it's not beliefs  whether we're or not we sort of realize  it when many Westerners are asked what  religion is that answer that it's about  belief in something a very Lutheran  Christian idea we celebrate holidays  they're a Christian we have symbols in  our windows during Christmas times that  are Christians but usually at that point  we say oh but they aren't religious and  maybe they don't carry the religious  meanings that they once did but they are  still a religious tradition that we rely  on and lean back on in times of crisis  we often fall back on our cultures  religious beliefs when many Swedes  passed away in the tsunami in 2004  priests were standing by the airport to  bless the bodies as they arrived when a  loved one passes we want a funeral and a  priest to save some words about it to  sort of reassure us this is common all  over the world  even those who sort of don't consider  themselves religious often fall back on  the religious traditions of our peers or  our family in times of crisis it's also  present in the way we tell stories and  thus how we conceive and understand the  world around us certain stories like the  passion story of Christ have left such a  mark on our cultural consciousness it is  programmed into our very views on the  world it is almost like second nature  Carl Jung very famously talked about the  archetypes of mythologies that all myths  follow different archetypes that are  sort of buried in the subconscious of  humanity  and while these theories are mostly  discarded by scholars of religion today  it does say something about our  relationship to stories stories shape us  and then we shape stories in turn as a  result it's this what has happened in  Batman vs Superman has this ingrained  story of Christ crept its way into the  film subconsciously through some kind of  cultural programming what then of the  cross is that also a kind of  subconscious accident probably not maybe  it's simply a coincidence that that  image appeared at that specific moment  and in that shot maybe it was indeed all  on purpose  none of the alternatives seemed all too  far-fetched to me or perhaps I'm simply  over analyzing everything like always  and there's nothing really interesting  thing going on here perhaps this is just  about a superhero alien from Krypton who  sacrifices himself for his two friends a  man who dresses up in a bad suit to  fight crime and gal gadot in any case it  did make for an interesting moment in an  otherwise let's face it dull film I'll  see you next time  [Music]

